#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050482 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705 :topic/math-academy-id 1998 :topic/title "Computing Areas Using a Change of Variables"}] :topic/id #uuid "4d32e47e-9f37-5a31-95c8-7aa1fca70d66" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq 4x+2y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u = 4x+2y$ and $v=y - x,$ write the following integral in terms of $u$ and $v.$ \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iint\\limits_D \\sin \\left(3(x+y)\\right)  \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\, \\mathrm{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/b8/b83369a26291155dbb8a5031d91fcf869cff259b7c170b6a15d58d317cee934d.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm d x \\, \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v))  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\mathrm d u \\, \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = 4x + 2y, \\qquad v = y - x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/46/461f793c9e8d6c3e5e5f67f846bade4acea9d40dc6c3975659d60167c33f6579.png)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables.\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu = 4x+2y, \\qquad v = y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{u-2v}6, \\qquad y =\\dfrac{u+4v}6.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(x+y))\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{u-2v}{6}+\\frac{u+4v}{6}))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(\\frac{2u+2v}{6}))|\\frac{1}{6}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}sin\\,(u+v)\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198015 :question/id #uuid "e8d75369-b381-53d3-b3af-37c6109c15a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12327" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{y-x}{x+y} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\: 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4 \\right\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n![](images/03/039187200f0587407c67e8663de037df0a9e71155e91c4ea497e9d10d66d9119.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y-x \\leq 1, \\qquad 1 \\leq x+y \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = y-x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad v = x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n![](images/e7/e7adae8ec845db2e091e9813a338b05895ebca800371df4841d327739863b863.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-x}{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{u}{v}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Domain"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198016 :question/id #uuid "1ad4ddff-b776-50d9-b698-c0172ee9d3ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-12328" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_D (2x+y)y^2 \\:\\mathrm{d}x \\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $\\left(\\dfrac32,0\\right) ,$ $\\left(\\dfrac52,0\\right) ,$ $(1,3),$ and $(0,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/03/039187200f0587407c67e8663de037df0a9e71155e91c4ea497e9d10d66d9119.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =  (2x+y)y^2\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\n\\qquad\nv=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/88/88c92ebc206481c3ef86661ee413358bb060cb85f642148c609de0bf1a9da2d1.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2},0) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+0,0) \\\\  & =(5,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2\\cdot 1+3,3) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(0,3) & =(2\\cdot 0+3,3) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq u \\leq 5, \\quad 0 \\leq v \\leq 3 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/c2/c296b1ee0451686608ec4b06c29d114ff1918233886d8471d3b2c118851aa25e.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral.\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y){y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Cases When the Transformation Is Inferred From the Integrand"}] :topic/math-academy-id 4132 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049639 :topic/math-academy-id 4151 :topic/title "Computing Triple Integrals Using a Change of Variables"}] :topic/title "Computing Double Integrals Using a Change of Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050508 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384 :topic/math-academy-id 1790 :topic/title "Areas of Parametric Surfaces"}] :topic/id #uuid "4f46c12e-9985-5c0f-879f-f155a81c2351" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172130 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171710 :question/id #uuid "38dc8ce4-246d-54bd-a400-48c801d9387f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12344" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ef5ddc89d7846d1126c60f34fc400669d23d6ca16c2b622addaf2916955ed27.svg)\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4\\sqrt{2}uv\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172131 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711 :question/id #uuid "fce43073-3f48-5e12-a5c0-ecef3a31a57a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12346" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} (x-y)\\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx = u+v, \\qquad \ny = u-v, \\qquad \nz = v\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x-y\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2v\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,2v\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3386 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883 :topic/math-academy-id 1264 :topic/title "Surface Integrals Over Cartesian Surfaces"}] :topic/title "Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050523 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049155 :topic/math-academy-id 1493 :topic/title "Graphing Tangent and Cotangent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/math-academy-id 725 :topic/title "Even and Odd Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "5049bff8-1875-5713-9a67-c3aa9836de2c" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175695 :question/id #uuid "2f4571af-99cf-5a4a-9998-fb6a0f7d6be9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11004" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The zeros of the function are at $x = \\pi n.$\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x = 2\\pi n.$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm2\\pi,\\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has an asymptote at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2$ and the period is $\\pi.$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x = \\dfrac \\pi 2 + \\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176050 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175696 :question/id #uuid "f4649856-d777-5468-a7f4-df50479e36d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11005" :question/problem "If $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2$ for some value of $x,$ then find the value of $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\tan x$ is $\\pi.$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\tan (x+n\\pi).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x & =tan⁡(x+(-2)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan\\left( x\\right) = -2,$ we get that $\\tan\\left( x-2\\pi\\right) = -2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175704 :question/id #uuid "0b146642-914c-52ce-9939-86752e95dcd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11013" :question/problem "If $\\tan x = -\\dfrac{2}{3},$ find the value of $\\tan (-x+7\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan{x}.$\n\n![](images/df/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\tan{x}$ is $\\pi.$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\tan{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\tan(-x) = -\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x+7\\pi ) & =tan⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 7) \\\\  & =tan⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\tan{x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡(-x) & =-tan⁡x \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{2}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\tan (-x+7\\pi)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Tangent Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3558 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050427 :topic/math-academy-id 3566 :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function"}] :topic/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191 :topic/math-academy-id 1115 :topic/title "Selecting Procedures for Calculating Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293 :topic/math-academy-id 803 :topic/title "Graphing Curves Defined Parametrically"}] :topic/id #uuid "509b8ea0-3cc6-5f94-8f44-a3e6445a83e5" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083054 :question/id #uuid "15c029df-b002-51cc-87db-a94d40a02771" :question/math-academy-id "e-595" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 798 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048727 :topic/math-academy-id 431 :topic/title "Second Derivatives of Parametric Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999 :topic/math-academy-id 4139 :topic/title "Differentiating Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162 :topic/math-academy-id 799 :topic/title "Calculating Tangent and Normal Lines with Parametric Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050787 :topic/math-academy-id 1043 :topic/title "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860 :topic/math-academy-id 997 :topic/title "The Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :topic/title "Differentiating Parametric Curves"}]}
